[38;2;124;124;124mNow consider the sum 1 + b. This is in S(A,B). Is it also in S(A,C)? That would [0m[38;2;124;124;124mrequire some a' ∈ A, c' ∈ C with a' + c' = 1 + b. Since c' ≥ c > b ≥ 4, we have [0m[38;2;124;124;124ma' = 1 + b - c' ≤ 1 + b - c < 1 + b - b = 1, so a' < 1, impossible. So 1+b is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnot in S(A,C). [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIs 1+b in S(B,C)? That would require b' ∈ B, c' ∈ C with b' + c' = 1 + b. Since [0m[38;2;124;124;124mc' ≥ c > b, b' = 1 + b - c' < 1, impossible. So 1+b is not in S(B,C). Good.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow consider sum 2 + b (if 2 ∈ A). This is in S(A,B). Is it in S(A,C)? Need a' +[0m[38;2;124;124;124mc' = 2 + b with a' ∈ A, c' ∈ C. c' ≥ c, so a' = 2 + b - c' ≤ 2 + b - c. If c > [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mb, then a' < 2, so a' = 1, meaning c' = 1 + b. Is 1 + b ∈ C? If not, then 2+b ∉ [0m[38;2;124;124;124mS(A,C).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIs 2+b in S(B,C)? Need b' + c' = 2 + b with b' ∈ B, c' ∈ C. c' ≥ c > b, so b' = [0m[38;2;124;124;124m2 + b - c' < 2, so b' = 1, but 1 ∈ A, not B. So 2+b ∉ S(B,C). Good.[0m

